  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nNo major changes from the previous forecast. An impressive \nsystem will bring periods of rain across the region into \nThursday. No flooding is expected, but there will be noticeable \nrises on the main stem rivers. Looking ahead, dry weather will \nsettle in on Friday and last for most of next week.\n\nEarly afternoon satellite and radar imagery showed several\ncirculations within an upper level low that was centered across\nthe Nebraska/Iowa/Missouri border. This PV anomaly was producing\nclouds and scattered showers across much of the Midwest. Closer\nto home, and area of deformation precipitation was beginning to\ntake shape across the southern half of Minnesota into northern\nWisconsin. This band will slowly lift northwest this evening. \nOn Wednesday, the main circulation will lift northward along the\nMississippi River valley, and this will bring additional \nrainfall across the region. On Wednesday evening, the \ndeformation band will stall out, and pivot back to the\nsoutheast, brining more precipitation across Minnesota and\nWisconsin. On Thursday, this system will slowly exit the region,\nand rain ends from west to east. By the time it ends, most\nlocations will see another half to an inch of rain, with the\nhighest amounts north of I-94 and west of I-35.\n\nLooking ahead, a prolonged stretch of dry weather will settle in\nacross the region. Temperatures will be cool Friday and\nSaturday, but then gradually warm up next week, with highs back\ninto the mid to upper 50s.",
